.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt RECV 2
.Os RavenBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm recv
.Nd receive a scheme-server request
.Sh SYNOPSIS
See the
.Pa raven-abi
crate (syscall number 0x68).
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
pops the next request queued for a process that registered a scheme
.Pq Xr scheme_register 2
and writes its 440-byte packet to
.Fa packet_address
.Fa capacity
must be at least that size.
The packet's
.Va buffer_addr ,
.Va buffer_len ,
.Va response_addr
and
.Va response_len
describe the request's captured buffers in the server's address space.
The returned handle is a server-side request handle; pass it to
.Xr send 2
to complete the request.
.Pp
The captured buffers are kernel-owned copies of the client range, not mappings of the client's
frames.  A
.Dv READ
capture is writable and is copied back to the client on completion when the client's mapping
is unchanged; a
.Dv WRITE
or
.Dv CALL
request capture is read-only for the server.
The server sees the bytes as they were when the syscall started.
.Pp
The destination address is validated when a request is dequeued.  If it is not a valid user
range, the request is completed with
.Er EPROTO
(so its client never waits forever) and
.Nm
returns
.Er EFAULT .
A request whose client exited while it was queued is skipped and its capture is released.
.Pp
.Nm
blocks until a request is available.
An empty scheme registration list returns
.Er EINVAL .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
A request handle is returned on success.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa capacity
is smaller than one packet, or the caller has no registered scheme.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa packet_address
is not a valid user destination.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr send 2 ,
.Xr call 2 ,
.Xr scheme 7
.Sh STANDARDS
RavenBSD ABI v1.
